WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.570
Narrator: If you peel back the layers

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.920
of Rapunzel's hair in "Tangled,"

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.920
you'll see just how complicated
animating 3D hair can be.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.310
But back then, Disney had
mainly focused on straight hair,

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:16.350
building on its previous 2D looks.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.417
With "Encanto," the studio figured out

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.770
how to create coiled hair like Mirabel's

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.678
with natural movement.

00:00:22.678 --> 00:00:25.244
But the animators didn't stop there.

00:00:25.244 --> 00:00:28.810
"Encanto" made history as the
first Disney animated movie

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.785
to represent the full
range of hair textures,

00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:34.101
from 1A to 4C.

00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:37.130
Getting from here to here

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:40.130
required over a decade of innovation.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.700
The story begins with a
familiar storybook princess

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.713
who in 2010 was seen for
the first time in 3D.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.210
Disney's first major foray
into 3D hair animation

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:53.670
came with "Tangled."

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.210
Rapunzel's 70 feet of hair

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.000
was basically its own
character in the movie,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.160
pretty much breaking every
real-life law of motion,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.256
and not just because it was magical.

00:01:03.256 --> 00:01:05.620
That's a lot of hair.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.640
She's growing it out.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.106
Nadim: Every shot of every movie

00:01:09.106 --> 00:01:11.798
has a lot of bending the laws of physics.

00:01:11.798 --> 00:01:14.490
Otherwise, things would look very flat.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.431
Narrator: This emphasizes a key tenet

00:01:16.431 --> 00:01:18.289
of Disney's animated hair.

00:01:18.289 --> 00:01:19.775
The goal isn't always to make it

00:01:19.775 --> 00:01:23.330
as realistic as possible,
but rather believable

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.630
within the fictional world of the story.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.805
To make Rapunzel's CG-animated locks

00:01:27.805 --> 00:01:30.505
look as appealing as
Disney's hand-drawn ones,

00:01:30.505 --> 00:01:33.295
the filmmakers started with a "hair bible"

00:01:33.295 --> 00:01:35.620
created by artist Glen
Keane, who was behind

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.950
some of the biggest hair
hits of Disney's 2D past.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.515
The bible set rules,
like how Rapunzel's hair

00:01:41.515 --> 00:01:44.720
could never fall in anything
resembling a straight line.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:45.986
It had to have volume;

00:01:45.986 --> 00:01:48.402
rhythmic curves, twists, and turns;

00:01:48.402 --> 00:01:50.660
and a signature swoop in the front.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.522
But that shampoo-commercial hair

00:01:52.522 --> 00:01:55.220
wouldn't be so easy to replicate in 3D.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.450
Nadim: It's not hand-drawn,
where you're focusing

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.298
more on the shaping and you could cheat.

00:02:00.298 --> 00:02:02.050
You have to kind of take
everything into account

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.741
when you're doing CG hair,

00:02:03.741 --> 00:02:05.416
even stuff that's not on screen.

00:02:05.416 --> 00:02:08.280
Narrator: Like wind or different
sources of light or shadow.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.690
And Rapunzel's strands
interacted with the environment

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.730
in ways never seen before.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.710
You had hair interacting with cloth,

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.890
with skin, with other hair.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.800
The other characters were
constantly touching, pulling,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.710
climbing, and rolling in it.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.605
Accounting for all these interactions

00:02:23.605 --> 00:02:26.552
would require simulation,
a way of automating

00:02:26.552 --> 00:02:29.600
the movement of elements
like hair, fur, and cloth.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.240
The only movie before "Tangled"

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.610
where I think we had really
even attempted simulated hair

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.060
was "Bolt" with Penny.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.127
We knew we had a huge task ahead of us

00:02:38.127 --> 00:02:39.660
to go from basically that

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.820
to 70-foot-long flowing hair.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.753
Narrator: Engineers then created a program

00:02:44.753 --> 00:02:46.711
called Dynamic Wires,

00:02:46.711 --> 00:02:49.595
which combined physically based simulation

00:02:49.595 --> 00:02:51.778
with laws for determining
the hair's behavior

00:02:51.778 --> 00:02:53.536
that defied physics.

00:02:53.536 --> 00:02:55.962
This allowed the artists
to make Rapunzel's hair

00:02:55.962 --> 00:02:58.770
twist and turn in exactly
the ways they wanted.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.450
In real life, this hair
would weigh 60 to 80 pounds,

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.220
so it'd clump into a mass
or drag on the ground,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.480
like a heavy tail.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.100
But in the movie, you see
it gliding smoothly along.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.485
Meanwhile, to give the artists more power

00:03:12.485 --> 00:03:14.690
to sculpt the look of Rapunzel's hair,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:18.300
the team broke down her 140,000 strands

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.160
into 147 different tubes.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.133
Michelle: The idea was
to sculpt tubes of hair

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:27.130
that would represent
the main blocks of hair.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.880
That process allowed us to kind of control

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.650
the way the hair would
break apart and interpolate.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:33.766
Narrator: This tube-grooming tool

00:03:33.766 --> 00:03:35.607
was the predecessor to Tonic,

00:03:35.607 --> 00:03:38.800
the hair-grooming software
that Disney still uses today.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.750
Nadim: A lot of the
technology from that movie

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.916
pretty much still exists till this day

00:03:43.916 --> 00:03:46.766
or has evolved into a newer form.

00:03:46.766 --> 00:03:48.690
Narrator: You can see
that clearly in "Frozen,"

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.518
which had over 50 unique hairstyles.

00:03:51.518 --> 00:03:53.520
Believe it or not, Elsa was originally

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.320
going to have black, spiky short hair.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.711
But as the characters evolved,

00:03:57.711 --> 00:04:00.370
Disney decided to give both Elsa and Anna

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.189
light-colored braids, in line with

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:05.670
the Norwegian cultural traditions
that inspired the movie.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.402
Michelle: We were pulling
the hair from her head,

00:04:07.402 --> 00:04:10.100
weaving it through into a
braid all the way to the end.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.360
And just trying to ensure
that those braid pieces

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:14.999
didn't crash into each other

00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:18.980
and would bend and move
properly was a challenge for us.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.621
Narrator: For "Bolt," Disney had developed

00:04:20.621 --> 00:04:23.004
a hair-brushing tool called iGroom,

00:04:23.004 --> 00:04:25.710
which worked well on
short-haired characters.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.250
But that plus the tube
tools from "Tangled"

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.590
weren't enough for braided looks,

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.580
so Disney's engineers built
a new hair-grooming system

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.840
called Tonic.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.040
Tonic is a volume-based tool,

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.048
which lets artists group the hairs

00:04:39.048 --> 00:04:41.220
on a character's head and move and direct

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.850
those sections of hair
in the desired ways.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.830
This allowed look artists like Michelle

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.671
to create the first
versions of complex styles

00:04:48.671 --> 00:04:51.346
within a few days, a process that before

00:04:51.346 --> 00:04:53.260
would've taken several weeks.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.155
The team was also able to use Tonic

00:04:55.155 --> 00:04:57.390
for the hair on the wolves and horses

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.156
and the shaggy reindeer
hair on Sven's neck.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.320
Elsa's hair had another
environmental element

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.470
to adapt to: snow.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:09.860
Michelle: Particles of snow
or sparkles on top of hair

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.769
is like procedural geometry
on top of procedural geometry.

00:05:12.769 --> 00:05:14.228
So that was hard to figure out.

00:05:14.228 --> 00:05:16.179
Narrator: And then there
were the gusts of wind.

00:05:16.179 --> 00:05:19.650
Michelle: Very stylized
bang shapes that, you know,

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.180
these kind of pieces that formed

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.940
that really distinctive silhouette.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.490
She really does, towards
the end of the movie,

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:26.860
get blown around quite a bit,

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.330
and trying to balance
maintaining that stylization

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.637
and that kind of appealing shape language

00:05:32.637 --> 00:05:35.820
with real physical motion.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.427
Narrator: Figuring out hair's interaction

00:05:37.427 --> 00:05:39.293
with the wintry elements in "Frozen"

00:05:39.293 --> 00:05:41.576
paid off in "Moana," where the focus

00:05:41.576 --> 00:05:44.120
was on hair's interaction
with more forces,

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.270
like water and character movements.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.040
Things might have been
more straightforward

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.148
if the demigod Maui had been bald,

00:05:50.148 --> 00:05:51.531
like The Rock himself,

00:05:51.531 --> 00:05:53.170
which was the original plan.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.540
But Polynesian cultural
advisors pointed out

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.700
that Maui's long hair is a
source of his spiritual energy,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.378
so both Maui and Moana ended
up with long, curly-wavy hair.

00:06:04.378 --> 00:06:06.940
I've only been thinking
of keeping this hair silky

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.290
and being awesome again.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:10.895
Narrator: The first task was sculpting

00:06:10.895 --> 00:06:13.413
their zigzag, or S-shaped, curls,

00:06:13.413 --> 00:06:16.380
a hair shape Disney hadn't created before.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.970
Michelle: Making those shapes
on very, very long hair

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:20.528
and then trying to
figure out how to manage

00:06:20.528 --> 00:06:22.297
those individual curl locks so they don't

00:06:22.297 --> 00:06:25.240
poke through each other
and catch on each other.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.598
Narrator: This task required Disney

00:06:26.598 --> 00:06:29.181
to expand Tonic's tube-grooming tool,

00:06:29.181 --> 00:06:31.680
giving it the ability to curl the hair up.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.820
After sculpting the shape,
the team figured out

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.470
how the waves would move
and hold their look.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.540
Nadim: Part of the trick
with something like wavy hair

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.410
or curly hair is retaining
the volume of the hair.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:42.956
Because if you just sim it as is,

00:06:42.956 --> 00:06:45.650
it'll just collapse and
fall flat on her head.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:48.133
So how do you retain the flowiness of it?

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.644
Narrator: The team
developed what they called

00:06:50.644 --> 00:06:52.235
an elastic rod model,

00:06:52.235 --> 00:06:54.270
which determined the
degree to which the hair

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.470
would retain its twists and springiness

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:59.070
under different forces,
like wind or water.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:02.350
Nadim: If, let's say, Moana
is falling through the sky

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:03.887
and her hair's really stretched, well,

00:07:03.887 --> 00:07:06.815
how much of her hair is going
to be a full straight line

00:07:06.815 --> 00:07:08.400
versus how much curl is going to be there?

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.243
Or if she compresses, how
much is it going to bunch up?

00:07:11.243 --> 00:07:13.510
Narrator: But Disney also
wanted to give its animators

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.360
an ability to guide the
simulation of the hair.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.610
So the engineers built a new
hair program, Quicksilver,

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:22.080
that combined rigging
and grooming controls.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.800
Instead of animating the
characters with static hair,

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.020
now, the animators could put
the hair into starting poses,

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.300
and Quicksilver's engine
would use those poses

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.990
to determine the resulting movement.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:36.150
By allowing artists to shape
the posing of the hair,

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.950
Disney was able to recover
some of the expressiveness

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:42.510
of hand-drawn animation that
could often get lost in CG.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.560
It's particularly useful
for the interaction moments,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.401
where the character is doing something

00:07:47.401 --> 00:07:49.189
with their hair specifically,

00:07:49.189 --> 00:07:51.650
and the animator wants to
guide what that's going to be.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:53.280
Narrator: They wanted Moana in particular

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.000
to be able to constantly
play with her hair,

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.470
since that habit is typical of teenagers,

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.030
as they observed it in
actor Auli'i Cravalho

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.750
as she performed Moana's
lines in the studio.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.252
The characters' darker hair

00:08:05.252 --> 00:08:07.030
also broke new ground for Disney.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.017
Nadim: If you look at
kind of previous movies,

00:08:09.017 --> 00:08:11.553
"Tangled," "Frozen,"
we haven't really done

00:08:11.553 --> 00:08:13.810
any black, darkish hair colors.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.340
So that reacts fairly differently to light

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:17.480
than other hair colors,

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:20.100
and how do you kind of
still show its richness?

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.333
You kind of have to have a movie

00:08:21.333 --> 00:08:22.974
that needs a hair color
to then be able to see

00:08:22.974 --> 00:08:25.338
how far your technology
goes and then tune to that.

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:27.311
And now we're at a pretty good spot

00:08:27.311 --> 00:08:29.686
with actually the shader
being able to handle

00:08:29.686 --> 00:08:31.719
a wide range of hair colors.

00:08:31.719 --> 00:08:33.610
Narrator: All of these
technologies and more

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:35.680
came into play in "Encanto."

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.450
The shading advancements from "Moana"

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.780
made it possible to get
the rich shades of hair

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.302
in the Madrigal family,

00:08:41.302 --> 00:08:43.951
and the S-shaped curls
seen on Moana and Maui

00:08:43.951 --> 00:08:46.293
appeared on some characters in "Encanto."

00:08:46.293 --> 00:08:48.768
Jose: We had the software to be able to do

00:08:48.768 --> 00:08:51.176
type 1 hair, type 2 hair very easily,

00:08:51.176 --> 00:08:52.984
but we hadn't really figured out

00:08:52.984 --> 00:08:56.461
how to do coils that are actually helical

00:08:56.461 --> 00:08:58.940
and that actually look like springs.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:00.980
Specifically for Mirabel,

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:05.860
she had kind of a type 3 curly hair,

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.100
like, loose ringlets
that get kind of tighter

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.590
in certain places.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.173
Narrator: The team added this tighter type

00:09:12.173 --> 00:09:13.660
of coil into Tonic.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:14.851
Jose: So there was a lot of collaboration

00:09:14.851 --> 00:09:15.942
with the technology team

00:09:15.942 --> 00:09:18.810
trying to figure out what
is hair actually doing

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.840
when it starts to coil
versus when it's wavy

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.060
and then figuring out
how we can get our tools

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:25.880
to actually do that.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:27.520
Narrator: Emphasizing
the unique attributes

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.410
of each hair type was a
big part of Jose's job

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.810
as a character look development artist.

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:33.968
Jose: We're trying to figure out,

00:09:33.968 --> 00:09:36.493
what naturally is beautiful
about this type of hair

00:09:36.493 --> 00:09:37.987
and how can we emphasize that?

00:09:37.987 --> 00:09:40.070
Narrator: And the diversity
goes all the way down

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:42.670
to the individual hairs
on a character's head.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:45.718
Curl direction is very important.

00:09:45.718 --> 00:09:48.470
Because you don't want two
curls to look exactly the same,

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.559
because then it feels very artificial.

00:09:50.559 --> 00:09:52.940
In everybody's hair,
there's a lot of variation.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:57.408
Things like variety in size
of the curls, hair color.

00:09:57.408 --> 00:10:00.620
We try to make sure that
nothing is symmetrical.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.610
Narrator: Every strand
of hair also figured in

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.593
to the dance sequences of "Encanto,"

00:10:04.593 --> 00:10:06.768
building on the movement work in "Moana."

00:10:06.768 --> 00:10:08.590
The artists started by looking at

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:10.300
a lot of reference material,

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.120
including footage of the choreography.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.290
Michelle: We knew that Mirabel and Luisa

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:17.550
and a lot of the characters
were going to be really active

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:20.910
and jumping around and in
a musical fantasy sequence

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.800
that they could be hanging upside down.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.192
Sometimes in those tests,
you find out that, like,

00:10:25.192 --> 00:10:28.567
one piece of hair is quite
a bit longer than the other.

00:10:28.567 --> 00:10:30.480
And so you have to go
back in and adjust it.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:31.813
Narrator: The team would
have to look at whether

00:10:31.813 --> 00:10:34.188
all the strands of hair reacted naturally

00:10:34.188 --> 00:10:37.820
to the character's
movements and to each other.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:41.100
It was important as ever to
honor differences in textures

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:42.400
for every character.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.450
Previously, Disney princesses
had mostly straight hair

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:47.930
that moved in big, sweeping paths.

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:49.640
To make more tightly curled hair

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.410
move naturally in "Encanto,"

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.480
the team had to adjust this approach.

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:56.560
Edna: We used to talk about
how, when they were dancing,

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.850
how the hair would have to move,

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.588
how the hair would have to perform.

00:11:00.588 --> 00:11:02.910
For example, we have
the idea that Afro hair,

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:05.670
or African hair, has not movement.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:08.240
And we have the perception
that that's something bad,

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.510
but that's not bad. It's just our hair.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:12.937
Our hair doesn't have a lot of movement.

00:11:12.937 --> 00:11:16.040
It's OK that it stay like that, you know?

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.713
So it doesn't have to be a ponytail

00:11:18.713 --> 00:11:20.550
with straight hair to be beautiful.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:23.100
Narrator: What set "Encanto"
apart from previous movies

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.200
was also the sheer scale
of its hair diversity,

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.650
not just for the Madrigal
family but for the entire town.

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.680
Edna: We have the 12 hair textures

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.580
in the 12 chapters of the family,

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:35.330
but also we have different styles

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:37.010
in the whole town in "Encanto."

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:38.130
You can see turbans.

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.070
You can see other type of braids.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.778
More indigenous population,
for example, in Colombia.

00:11:42.778 --> 00:11:45.750
There's a little girl in the town,

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:49.737
she has an Afro not like this
with turban, but all free.

00:11:49.737 --> 00:11:52.500
And also you can find women with braids,

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:54.990
very Colombian and African style.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:56.147
Narrator: Every single head of hair

00:11:56.147 --> 00:11:59.470
had to be styled
meticulously by the artists,

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:01.700
picking up where they
left off with "Frozen."

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.700
Nadim: So if you watch
kind of the evolution

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.679
of having straight-hair characters,

00:12:05.679 --> 00:12:06.870
and then suddenly "Encanto"

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.617
has all these crowd
characters with braids,

00:12:08.617 --> 00:12:11.490
and we could barely do two-braid
characters on "Frozen."

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.365
So the advancements are really there,

00:12:13.365 --> 00:12:14.498
and they trickle down.

00:12:14.498 --> 00:12:15.950
Narrator: But at the start of production,

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:18.970
braiding hair was still
a very manual process.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.890
Jose: So, like, how you would
actually braid actual hair

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:24.460
in real life, we have to do that

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:28.620
with essentially 3D tubes
that we use in our computer.

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.003
By the end of the movie,

00:12:30.003 --> 00:12:33.020
we had a more automatic
process for making braids

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:36.136
where you just draw or you create a curve,

00:12:36.136 --> 00:12:40.250
a line along the head where you
want your braid to come out,

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:43.350
and then it'll do a little
computer-made braid for you.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.400
Narrator: That doesn't mean
all the work is finished.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:49.250
Jose: There's so much diversity,
even within braid types,

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:52.074
that then there's more complex braids

00:12:52.074 --> 00:12:54.274
that we're looking at to try to figure out

00:12:54.274 --> 00:12:55.760
how to make those look really good.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.593
Narrator: Ultimately,
"Encanto" made history

00:12:57.593 --> 00:13:00.300
as the first Disney
animated movie to represent

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:04.290
the full range of hair
textures, from 1A to 4C;

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:07.400
a milestone reached by
building a foundation of tools

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:08.620
and then adapting them.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.313
Nadim: What's also great about these tools

00:13:10.313 --> 00:13:12.205
is we're able to repurpose them

00:13:12.205 --> 00:13:13.762
in areas that you might not expect.

00:13:13.762 --> 00:13:17.030
The system that we use to
do hair is the same system

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.120
that we use to do plenty of other things,

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.130
like Mirabel's dress.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:24.050
Her skirt has tons and
tons of embroidery on it.

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:25.740
We were able to use iGroom to be able

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:28.020
to do some of the embroidery.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:29.195
Narrator: The technological progress

00:13:29.195 --> 00:13:31.936
is impressive on its
own, but it's always done

00:13:31.936 --> 00:13:34.690
in service of telling bigger stories.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:36.092
Michelle: I think now
at this point we have

00:13:36.092 --> 00:13:37.599
a really complete set of tools,

00:13:37.599 --> 00:13:39.616
and we should be able
to make and represent

00:13:39.616 --> 00:13:41.090
the panoply of humanity,

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:43.490
which is a really good place to be.

